## Overview
Rewrite the EventBus interfaces to no longer be aware of Systems (in particular `SystemBase`), and favour only callback registration., in order to break dependencies for modularisation.

We solve the problem about not being able to identify callbacks by introducing a `NetworkEventRegistration` type, which asks for an identifier ID which is used as the discriminator. By doing this, we also enable more powerful api usage, such as clients registering multiple times for the same event, or grouping/managing events under specific IDs. This isn't the point of the change, but it's a nice side-effect nonetheless. Some API is added to support this.

Finally, we change the way the deserialization works such that the EventBus is aware of how to deserialize the events that require custom deserialization. Doing this allows us to pass a polymorphic `EventData` object as the exchange type via the callbacks. This wraps `ReplicatedValue`, which is moved to common and is now usable as an interop type. We also make it structurally clear that the custom-deserialization types are special cases.

Also rewrote the event bus test suite from scratch, as its simpler interfaces can now be served by a simpler test suite.

## Reviewer Notes
Scary amount of files, but half of them are merely renamespacing `ReplicatedValue` in the first commit.
I'm experimenting with showing the tests before the implementation, in a sort of "Here's what we're trying to achieve" sort of way. Let me know what you think.

Due to some of the event bus files being almost entirely rewritten, I imagine this will be hard to read if you are using the unified diff view. 

> [!IMPORTANT]  
> Pay special attention to the second to last commit, which is dealing with the quite confused hotspot sequence issue, and working around lack of RTTI in WASM. (Which the need for was in itself a workaround for an event design that makes little intuitive sense to my mind.) 
>
> The polymorphic approach that requires `dynamic_casts` for Sequences and HotspotSequences gets replaced in this final commit. Keep that in mind. I've left this in rather than rebasing it away because I think it's clearer to see it all done independently in reaction to the RTTI discovery.

## Breaking Changes
- `EventBus` renamed to `NetworkEventBus` in order to clarify what it does, and reduce conflation with `EventSystem`
- `ReplicatedValue` moved from csp::multiplayer to csp::common
- "Params" objects are now polymorphic `EventData` objects.
  - `AssetDetailBlobParams` -> `AssetDetailBlobChangedEventData`
  - `ConversationEventParams` -> `ConversationEventData`
  - `UserPermissionsParams` -> `AccessControlChangedEventData`
  - `SequenceChangedParams` -> `SequenceChangedEventData` (with null `HotspotData` member)
  - `SequenceHotspotChangedParams` -> `SequenceChangedEventData` (with non-null `HotspotData` member)
- `EventBus` implementation has changed significantly.
  - `csp::multiplayer::EventBus::ParameterisedCalbackHandler` now known as `csp::multiplayer::NetworkEventCallback`
  - `NetworkEventCallback` is now provided a potentially polymorphic `EventData` object.
  - Listening/StopListening methods now take a new `csp::multiplayer::NetworkEventRegistration` object to facilitate better management of events
  - Using `NetworkEventRegistration`, users may now register to recieve the same event in multiple locations, and manage event registration/deregistration more broadly.
  - Most EventBus methods now return a bool to convey success.
  
 ## Example
  The best examples are the tests, but briefly:
  
```cpp
  // Listening for an event
   NetworkEventBus->ListenNetworkEvent(NetworkEventRegistration("MyReceiverId", "MyEventName"),
     [](const EventData& EventData) { /*Do something with EventData.EventValues*/ });
```
  
```cpp
  // Stop listening to an event
 NetworkEventBus->StopListenNetworkEvent(NetworkEventRegistration { "MyReceiverId", "MyEventName" });
```
  
```cpp
  // Listening for the same event multiple times/in multiple locations
   NetworkEventBus->ListenNetworkEvent(NetworkEventRegistration("MyReceiverId1", "MyEventName"),
     [](const EventData& EventData) { /*Do something with EventData.EventValues*/ });
   NetworkEventBus->ListenNetworkEvent(NetworkEventRegistration("MyReceiverId2", "MyEventName"),
     [](const EventData& EventData) { /*Do something with EventData.EventValues*/ });
```
  
```cpp
  // Listening for many events
   NetworkEventBus->ListenNetworkEvent(NetworkEventRegistration("MyReceiverId", "MyEventName"),
     [](const EventData& EventData) { /*Do something with EventData.EventValues*/ });
   NetworkEventBus->ListenNetworkEvent(NetworkEventRegistration("MyReceiverId", "MyOtherEventName"),
     [](const EventData& EventData) { /*Do something with EventData.EventValues*/ });
```
  
```cpp
  // Stop listening to a tranche of events
  NetworkEventBus->StopListenAllNetworkEvents("MyReceiverId");
```
  
```cpp
  // Get info on all registered events
const csp::common::Array<NetworkEventRegistration> AllRegistrations = EventBus->AllRegistrations();
```

```cpp
//NetworkEventData definition
class NetworkEventData
{
public:
    /// @brief The name of the event that sent this EventData
    csp::common::String EventName;
    /// @brief The ID of the client that sent this EventData
    uint64_t SenderClientId;
    /// @brief The collection of values sent with this Event. May be empty.
    csp::common::Array<csp::common::ReplicatedValue> EventValues;
};
```

> [!NOTE]  
> I wanted to provide some convenience methods to avoid users needing to always construct a `NetworkEventRegistration` explicitly, but of course, our old friend the wrapper generator gets in the way.

## Current state

The `ParameterParser` class is responsible for expanding parameters into `string`s. For each parameter, it accepts a few options to enable (or disable) some normalization steps (e.g. trimming spaces, expanding variables, or unescaping escape sequences).

Some parameters only allow a restricted set of string values, and encode the string into an int or bool, using native conversion (`intval`) or specific functions (`ListUtils::decode…`).

For example:

```php
$params = new ParameterParser( $frame, $rawParams, [
	'somestring'   => [ 'unescape' => true ],
	'somecsoption' => [],
	'someint'      => []
] );

$someString = $params->get( 'somestring' );
$someCSOption = $params->get( 'somecsoption' );
$someCSOption = ListUtils::decodeCSOption( $someCSOption );
$someInt = $params->get( 'someint' );
$someInt = is_numeric( $someInt ) ? intval( $someInt ) : 10;
```

## Proposed changes

Add an additional wikitext `formatter` step to the `ParameterParser` (doing nothing by default). It can be used to cast the string to another type, by providing a `WikitextFormatter` object with a `->format( $text )` method.

With the previous example:

```php
$params = new ParameterParser( $frame, $rawParams, [
	'somestring'   => [ 'unescape' => true ],
	'somecsoption' => [ 'formatter' => new BoolFormatter( 'cs', 'ncs' ) ],
	'someint'      => [ 'formatter' => new IntFormatter(), 'default' => 10 ]
] );

$someString = $params->get( 'somestring' );
$someCSOption = $params->get( 'somecsoption' );
$someInt = $params->get( 'someint' );
```

## Why

Unlike the current functions, this would allow to decode the same parameter differently depending on its name. For example, for #49, being able to make `{{#lstuniq:||| cs }}` an alias of `{{#lstuniq:||| uniquecs = yes }}` while their (bool) values are encoded differently (recognizing `cs`/`ncs` vs `yes`/`no`).

